// Compounds: [formula, name, bond type, what it is, uses, how it is made or where it comes from]
window.COMPOUNDS=[
['H2O','Water','covalent','Two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom in a bent shape. The slightly negative oxygen and positive hydrogens make water molecules stick together, which is why water has a high boiling point and dissolves so many things.','Drinking; every living cell; cooling; cleaning; hydro-electric power','Burning hydrogen: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Also made in every cell when food is “burned” for energy.'],
['H2O2','Hydrogen peroxide','covalent','Water with an extra oxygen. It breaks down easily and releases oxygen, which kills germs and bleaches.','Disinfecting wounds; bleaching hair and paper; rocket propellant','Made industrially from hydrogen and oxygen using a special catalyst.'],
['NaCl','Sodium chloride (table salt)','ionic','A cube-shaped crystal of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions. Sodium gives an electron to chlorine, and the opposite charges hold tight.','Food seasoning and preserving; de-icing roads; making chlorine and sodium hydroxide','Evaporating seawater or mining rock salt. In the lab: 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl (very violent).'],
['KCl','Potassium chloride','ionic','A salt much like table salt, with potassium instead of sodium.','Fertiliser (potash); low-sodium salt substitute; medicine','Mined from ancient dried seas.'],
['HCl','Hydrochloric acid','covalent','Hydrogen chloride gas dissolved in water — a strong acid.','Your stomach uses it to digest food; cleaning rust off steel; making PVC; controlling swimming-pool pH','H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl, then dissolved in water.'],
['NaOH','Sodium hydroxide (caustic soda)','ionic','A strong alkali (base). It is very corrosive to skin.','Making soap, paper and aluminium; drain cleaner; food processing','Passing electricity through salt water (the chlor-alkali process).'],
['CO2','Carbon dioxide','covalent','A straight molecule: O=C=O. A greenhouse gas that traps heat in the atmosphere.','Plants use it to make sugar; fizzy drinks; fire extinguishers; dry ice for cooling','Burning anything with carbon: C + O₂ → CO₂. We breathe it out.'],
['CO','Carbon monoxide','covalent','A poisonous gas with no smell. It sticks to haemoglobin 200 times more strongly than oxygen.','Making iron from ore in blast furnaces; making methanol and plastics','Burning carbon with too little oxygen. Carbon-monoxide alarms warn of it at home.'],
['CH4','Methane (natural gas)','covalent','One carbon with four hydrogens in a pyramid (tetrahedron) shape — the simplest hydrocarbon.','Heating and cooking; power stations; making hydrogen and ammonia','From underground gas fields, rotting plants, cows’ stomachs and wetlands. A strong greenhouse gas.'],
['C3H8','Propane','covalent','A hydrocarbon with three carbons; easy to squeeze into a liquid.','Camping gas, barbecues and heating (LPG)','Separated from natural gas and crude oil.'],
['C8H18','Octane (in petrol)','covalent','A chain of 8 carbons with hydrogens. Petrol is a mix of many hydrocarbons like it.','Fuel for cars','Distilling crude oil and reshaping molecules in refineries.'],
['C2H5OH','Ethanol (alcohol)','covalent','A small molecule with an –OH group, which makes it mix with water.','Drinks; hand sanitiser; fuel added to petrol; solvent','Yeast fermenting sugar: C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.'],
['CH3COOH','Acetic acid (vinegar)','covalent','A weak acid with a –COOH group. Vinegar is about 5% acetic acid in water.','Food flavour and pickling; making plastic (PET) and glue','Bacteria turning alcohol into vinegar, or from methanol and carbon monoxide.'],
['C6H12O6','Glucose','covalent','A ring-shaped sugar. The main fuel for cells, carried by the blood.','Energy for your body and brain; sports drinks','Plants make it by photosynthesis: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂.'],
['C12H22O11','Sucrose (table sugar)','covalent','Glucose joined to fructose.','Sweetening food; preserving jam','Extracted from sugar cane and sugar beet.'],
['C9H8O4','Aspirin','covalent','Acetylsalicylic acid — first made from a chemical found in willow bark.','Pain and fever relief; low doses help prevent heart attacks','Made from salicylic acid and acetic anhydride.'],
['C8H10N4O2','Caffeine','covalent','A bitter molecule with four nitrogen atoms that blocks the brain’s “tiredness” signal (adenosine).','In coffee, tea, cola and energy drinks','Made by coffee and tea plants to fend off insects; also made in factories.'],
['NH3','Ammonia','covalent','One nitrogen and three hydrogens. A sharp-smelling gas that dissolves easily in water.','Fertilisers (about 80%); cleaning products; refrigeration; making nylon and explosives','The Haber–Bosch process: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ at high pressure with an iron catalyst. It feeds about half the world.'],
['HNO3','Nitric acid','covalent','A strong acid that easily gives away oxygen.','Fertilisers (ammonium nitrate); explosives; etching metals','Burning ammonia with oxygen over a platinum catalyst (Ostwald process).'],
['H2SO4','Sulfuric acid','covalent','A strong, oily acid that grabs water. The most-produced industrial chemical in the world.','Fertilisers; car batteries; refining metals; making detergents, paints and dyes','Burning sulfur to SO₂, turning it into SO₃ with a vanadium catalyst, then adding water (contact process).'],
['H3PO4','Phosphoric acid','covalent','A mild acid with one phosphorus atom.','Fertilisers; cola flavour; rust remover; dentistry','Treating phosphate rock with sulfuric acid.'],
['NaHCO3','Sodium bicarbonate (baking soda)','ionic','A mild alkali that releases CO₂ bubbles when it meets acid.','Making cakes rise; antacid; fire extinguishers; cleaning','Solvay process from salt, limestone and ammonia.'],
['Na2CO3','Sodium carbonate (washing soda)','ionic','An alkali salt.','Making glass (about half of all use); softening water; detergents','Solvay process, or mined as trona.'],
['CaCO3','Calcium carbonate','ionic','The mineral in limestone, chalk, marble, seashells, coral and eggshells.','Building stone; cement and lime; chalk; toothpaste filler; antacid; calcium supplement','Sea creatures build shells from dissolved calcium and CO₂; the shells pile up as limestone over millions of years.'],
['CaO','Calcium oxide (quicklime)','ionic','A white powder that heats up strongly when mixed with water.','Cement; steel making; treating acidic soil; purifying sugar','Heating limestone: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. This step makes cement a large source of CO₂.'],
['Ca(OH)2','Calcium hydroxide (slaked lime)','ionic','A weak, cheap alkali.','Mortar and plaster; water treatment; limewater test for CO₂','CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂.'],
['CaSO4','Calcium sulfate (gypsum)','ionic','A soft white mineral that holds water in its crystals.','Plaster of Paris; plasterboard; plaster casts for broken bones; tofu making','Mined, or made from cleaning sulfur out of power-station smoke.'],
['MgO','Magnesium oxide','ionic','A very heat-resistant white solid.','Furnace linings; antacids; supplements','Burning magnesium (brilliant white flame): 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO.'],
['Mg(OH)2','Magnesium hydroxide','ionic','A weak alkali that neutralises acid.','Antacid (milk of magnesia); flame retardant','From seawater and lime.'],
['Al2O3','Aluminium oxide (alumina)','ionic','A very hard, heat-resistant oxide. Ruby and sapphire are alumina with a trace of chromium or iron/titanium.','Making aluminium metal; sandpaper; gems; spark-plug insulators; phone camera covers (sapphire)','Refined from bauxite (Bayer process).'],
['Fe2O3','Iron(III) oxide (rust)','ionic','Reddish-brown oxide. Rust forms when iron meets both water and oxygen.','Iron ore (haematite); red paint and pigments (Mars is red because of it); polishing powder','4Fe + 3O₂ + 2H₂O → rust (hydrated Fe₂O₃).'],
['Fe3O4','Magnetite','ionic','A black, naturally magnetic iron oxide.','Iron ore; the first compasses (lodestone); MRI contrast particles','Found in rocks; some bacteria and birds make tiny crystals of it to sense Earth’s magnetism.'],
['FeS2','Iron pyrite (fool’s gold)','ionic','Shiny brassy cubes that fooled gold hunters.','Source of sulfur and sulfuric acid; used to make sparks in early guns','Forms in rocks with iron and sulfur.'],
['CuSO4','Copper(II) sulfate','ionic','Bright blue crystals (with water); white when dried.','Killing fungi on grapes (Bordeaux mixture); algae control; testing for water; dyeing','Dissolving copper or copper oxide in sulfuric acid.'],
['CuO','Copper(II) oxide','ionic','A black powder.','Blue-green glazes; batteries; wood preservatives','Heating copper in air.'],
['ZnO','Zinc oxide','ionic','A white powder that blocks UV light.','Sunscreen; nappy cream; making rubber tyres; paint','Burning zinc vapour in air.'],
['SiO2','Silicon dioxide (silica, quartz, sand)','network','A giant 3-D network of silicon and oxygen atoms — very hard, with a high melting point.','Glass; concrete; sand for building; quartz crystals in watches; fibre optics','The most common mineral in Earth’s crust.'],
['SiC','Silicon carbide','network','A diamond-like network of silicon and carbon. Very hard.','Sandpaper and cutting discs; brake discs; power chips in electric cars','Heating sand with carbon at over 2,000 °C.'],
['TiO2','Titanium dioxide','ionic','The whitest, brightest pigment known.','White paint, paper, plastics and toothpaste; sunscreen; self-cleaning glass','Refined from rutile and ilmenite ores.'],
['NaClO','Sodium hypochlorite (bleach)','ionic','Releases chlorine that destroys germs and colours.','Household bleach; disinfecting water','Chlorine + sodium hydroxide.'],
['KNO3','Potassium nitrate (saltpetre)','ionic','An oxygen-rich salt.','Gunpowder (75%); fertiliser; preserving meat; toothpaste for sensitive teeth','Once scraped from caves and dung heaps; now made from KCl and nitric acid.'],
['NH4NO3','Ammonium nitrate','ionic','A salt with 35% nitrogen.','Fertiliser; mining explosives; instant cold packs','Ammonia + nitric acid.'],
['CO(NH2)2','Urea','covalent','A small molecule with two nitrogen groups — the body’s way of getting rid of extra nitrogen in urine.','The most used nitrogen fertiliser; making plastics and resins; skin creams','From ammonia and CO₂. It was the first natural molecule made in a lab (1828).'],
['SO2','Sulfur dioxide','covalent','A choking gas. In the air it forms acid rain.','Preserving wine and dried fruit; bleaching paper; making sulfuric acid','Burning sulfur or sulfur-containing coal and oil; volcanoes.'],
['NO2','Nitrogen dioxide','covalent','A brown poisonous gas that forms smog and acid rain.','Making nitric acid','Forms in hot car engines and power stations.'],
['N2O','Nitrous oxide (laughing gas)','covalent','A sweet-smelling gas.','Pain relief at the dentist and in childbirth; whipped-cream cans; racing engines','Heating ammonium nitrate. Also a strong greenhouse gas from fertilised fields.'],
['O3','Ozone','covalent','Three oxygen atoms. High up it shields us from UV; at ground level it is a lung irritant in smog.','Disinfecting water; removing smells','UV light or electric sparks split O₂; the pieces join other O₂ molecules.'],
['AgCl','Silver chloride','ionic','A white salt that darkens in light.','Photochromic (self-darkening) glasses; reference electrodes','Silver nitrate + salt water gives a white solid.'],
['AgNO3','Silver nitrate','ionic','A soluble silver salt.','Making mirrors; antiseptic; photography','Dissolving silver in nitric acid.'],
['KMnO4','Potassium permanganate','ionic','Deep purple crystals; a strong oxidiser.','Disinfecting water; treating skin conditions; lab tests','Made from manganese dioxide and potassium hydroxide.'],
['LiCoO2','Lithium cobalt oxide','ionic','Layers that lithium ions slide in and out of as a battery charges and discharges.','Positive electrode of phone and laptop batteries','Heating lithium carbonate with cobalt oxide.'],
['Li2CO3','Lithium carbonate','ionic','The main traded form of lithium.','Batteries; ceramics and glass; medicine for bipolar disorder','Evaporating lithium-rich brines in salt flats.'],
['MgSO4','Magnesium sulfate (Epsom salt)','ionic','A bitter salt.','Bath salts; fertiliser; medicine (magnesium deficiency, pre-eclampsia)','Found in mineral springs at Epsom, England.'],
['CaF2','Calcium fluoride (fluorite)','ionic','A mineral that glows under UV — the word “fluorescence” comes from it.','Source of fluorine; lenses for cameras and UV light; steel making','Mined.'],
['NaF','Sodium fluoride','ionic','A fluoride salt.','Toothpaste and water fluoridation to strengthen enamel','Neutralising hydrofluoric acid with sodium hydroxide.'],
['HF','Hydrofluoric acid','covalent','A weak but extremely dangerous acid that dissolves glass.','Etching glass and chips; making Teflon and refrigerants','Fluorite + sulfuric acid.'],
['C2H4','Ethylene (ethene)','covalent','Two carbons joined by a double bond — the building block of the most common plastic.','Making polyethylene and antifreeze; ripening bananas','Cracking crude oil or natural gas. Fruits release it naturally to ripen.'],
['(C2H4)n','Polyethylene (polythene)','covalent','Thousands of ethylene units linked in long chains — a polymer.','Plastic bags, bottles, food wrap, pipes (the most made plastic)','Polymerising ethylene with a catalyst.'],
['(C2H3Cl)n','PVC (polyvinyl chloride)','covalent','A chain polymer with chlorine on every second carbon — tough and fire-resistant.','Water pipes; window frames; cable insulation; flooring','Made from ethylene and chlorine.'],
['(C8H8)n','Polystyrene','covalent','A chain polymer with benzene rings sticking out. Foamed with gas, it becomes light packaging foam.','Packaging foam; disposable cups; CD cases','Polymerising styrene from oil.'],
['(C2F4)n','PTFE (Teflon)','covalent','A carbon chain completely covered in fluorine — almost nothing sticks to it.','Non-stick pans; waterproof fabrics; seals and bearings','Polymerising tetrafluoroethylene. Discovered by accident in 1938.'],
['C3H6O','Acetone','covalent','A small solvent molecule that evaporates quickly.','Nail-polish remover; paint thinner; making plastics','From propylene and benzene. Your body makes a little when burning fat.'],
['C6H8O7','Citric acid','covalent','The sour acid in lemons and limes.','Sour flavour in food and drinks; cleaning limescale; preservative','Made by the mould Aspergillus niger fed with sugar.'],
['GaN','Gallium nitride','covalent','A hard semiconductor that gives off blue light.','Blue and white LEDs; Blu-ray lasers; small fast phone chargers','Grown as thin crystal layers from gallium and ammonia gases.'],
['GaAs','Gallium arsenide','covalent','A fast semiconductor.','Solar cells on satellites; phone radio chips; infrared LEDs','Grown as crystals from gallium and arsenic.'],
['CdTe','Cadmium telluride','covalent','A semiconductor that absorbs sunlight well.','Thin-film solar panels','Deposited as thin layers on glass.'],
['WC','Tungsten carbide','covalent','Extremely hard — almost as hard as diamond.','Drill bits; cutting tools; ballpoint-pen tips; mining equipment','Heating tungsten powder with carbon.'],
['TiN','Titanium nitride','covalent','A gold-coloured ceramic coating.','Coating drills and tools; decorative “gold” finish; medical implants','Deposited as thin films.'],
['BN','Boron nitride','network','Two forms: a slippery white one like graphite, and a very hard one like diamond.','Lubricants; cosmetics; cutting tools','Boric acid heated with ammonia.'],
['ZnS','Zinc sulfide','ionic','A mineral that glows when hit by light or electrons (when a trace of copper is added).','Glow-in-the-dark paint; old TV screens; infrared windows','Mined as sphalerite.'],
['PbS','Lead sulfide (galena)','ionic','Shiny grey cubes — the main ore of lead.','Source of lead; early radio detectors (“crystal sets”)','Mined.'],
['HgS','Mercury sulfide (cinnabar)','ionic','A red mineral.','Source of mercury; ancient red pigment vermilion (toxic)','Mined.'],
['BaSO4','Barium sulfate','ionic','So insoluble it passes through the body unchanged.','X-ray “barium meal”; white paint filler; drilling mud','Mined as barite.'],
['SnO2','Tin(IV) oxide','ionic','A see-through conducting oxide when mixed with other elements.','Ore of tin; conductive glass; glazes','Mined as cassiterite.'],
['AgBr','Silver bromide','ionic','Turns dark where light hits it — the basis of film photography.','Photographic film and paper','Silver nitrate + potassium bromide.'],
['KI','Potassium iodide','ionic','A source of iodine.','Iodised salt; protecting the thyroid after nuclear accidents','Iodine + potassium hydroxide.'],
['SF6','Sulfur hexafluoride','covalent','A very heavy, unreactive gas that makes voices sound deep. A very strong greenhouse gas.','Insulating high-voltage electrical switches','Sulfur burned in fluorine.'],
['LiFePO4','Lithium iron phosphate','ionic','A safe, long-lasting battery material without cobalt or nickel.','Batteries for electric buses, cars and home solar storage','Made from lithium, iron and phosphate salts.'],
['CH3OH','Methanol','covalent','The simplest alcohol — poisonous to drink.','Making plastics and glues; fuel; antifreeze in windscreen wash','From natural gas via carbon monoxide and hydrogen.'],
['C6H6','Benzene','covalent','A flat ring of six carbons with shared electrons — very stable. It causes cancer, so it is handled carefully.','Starting point for plastics, nylon, dyes and medicines','From crude oil.'],
['NaNO3','Sodium nitrate (Chile saltpetre)','ionic','An oxygen-rich salt.','Fertiliser; preserving food; heat storage in solar power plants','Mined in the Atacama Desert.'],
['C17H35COONa','Sodium stearate (soap)','ionic','A long oily tail with a salty head — the tail grabs grease, the head grabs water.','Bar soap','Fat + sodium hydroxide (saponification).']
];

// Materials: [id, emoji, name, group, key elements, made from, how it is made, used for]
window.MATERIALS=[
['steel','🏗️','Steel','Metals & alloys',['Fe','C','Mn'],'Iron ore, coke (from coal), limestone, recycled scrap','Iron ore is melted with coke in a blast furnace; oxygen is blown through to burn off most carbon, leaving iron with 0.1–2% carbon. Electric furnaces melt scrap.','Buildings, bridges, cars, ships, tools, cans, rails'],
['stainless','🍴','Stainless steel','Metals & alloys',['Fe','Cr','Ni','C'],'Steel + chromium (at least 10.5%) + nickel','Chromium forms an invisible oxide skin that repairs itself, so it does not rust.','Cutlery, sinks, surgical tools, kitchen equipment'],
['bronze','🔔','Bronze','Metals & alloys',['Cu','Sn'],'Copper (about 88%) + tin (about 12%)','Melted together. Harder than copper alone — it started the Bronze Age about 5,000 years ago.','Bells, statues, medals, ship propellers, bearings'],
['brass','🎺','Brass','Metals & alloys',['Cu','Zn'],'Copper + zinc (usually 30–40%)','Melted together. Gold-coloured, easy to shape, does not spark.','Trumpets and horns, door handles, taps, bullet cases, keys'],
['solder','🔌','Lead-free solder','Metals & alloys',['Sn','Ag','Cu'],'Tin with a little silver and copper','Melts at about 217 °C to join electronic parts.','Every circuit board'],
['alualloy','✈️','Aircraft aluminium','Metals & alloys',['Al','Cu','Mg','Zn'],'Aluminium with copper, magnesium or zinc','Alloys are heat-treated so tiny particles form inside and make the metal much stronger.','Aircraft, bikes, car bodies, phone frames'],
['titanium','🦴','Titanium alloy (Ti-6Al-4V)','Metals & alloys',['Ti','Al','V'],'Titanium + 6% aluminium + 4% vanadium','Made in vacuum furnaces because hot titanium grabs oxygen and nitrogen.','Jet engines, hip and knee implants, racing bikes'],
['gold18','💍','18-carat gold','Metals & alloys',['Au','Ag','Cu'],'75% gold + silver and copper','Pure gold is too soft for jewellery; adding other metals makes it harder and changes its colour (rose, white).','Rings, watches, jewellery'],
['glass','🪟','Window glass','Building',['Si','O','Na','Ca'],'Sand (SiO₂) + soda ash (Na₂CO₃) + limestone (CaCO₃)','Melted at about 1,500 °C and floated on molten tin to make perfectly flat sheets.','Windows, bottles, jars, screens'],
['pyrex','🧪','Borosilicate glass','Building',['Si','B','O','Na'],'Sand + boron oxide','Boron makes the glass expand very little when heated, so it does not crack.','Lab glassware, oven dishes, telescope mirrors'],
['concrete','🧱','Cement & concrete','Building',['Ca','Si','Al','Fe','O'],'Limestone + clay → cement; cement + sand + gravel + water → concrete','Limestone and clay are baked at 1,450 °C into “clinker” and ground to cement. With water it forms crystals that glue sand and stones together.','Buildings, roads, bridges, dams — the most-used material after water'],
['ceramic','🏺','Brick & porcelain','Building',['Si','Al','O','K'],'Clay (aluminium silicates) + feldspar + quartz','Shaped wet, then fired in a kiln so the particles fuse.','Bricks, tiles, plates, toilets, insulators'],
['pe','🛍️','Polyethylene (plastic)','Plastics',['C','H'],'Ethylene from oil or natural gas (or sugar cane)','Small ethylene molecules are linked into very long chains (polymerisation).','Bags, bottles, food wrap, toys, pipes'],
['pvc','🚰','PVC','Plastics',['C','H','Cl'],'Ethylene + chlorine from salt','Vinyl chloride molecules are linked into chains.','Pipes, window frames, cables, flooring'],
['nylon','🧵','Nylon','Plastics',['C','H','N','O'],'Chemicals from oil','Two kinds of molecules join end to end, like a chain of paper clips, then the melt is pulled into fibres.','Clothes, ropes, toothbrushes, gears'],
['pet','🥤','PET','Plastics',['C','H','O'],'Chemicals from oil (ethylene glycol + terephthalic acid)','Joined into chains and blown into bottle shapes. Easy to recycle.','Drink bottles, polyester clothes, food trays'],
['ptfe','🍳','PTFE (Teflon)','Plastics',['C','F'],'Fluorite + chloroform → tetrafluoroethylene','Linked into chains fully wrapped in fluorine, so nothing sticks.','Non-stick pans, waterproof jackets, seals'],
['silicone','🧁','Silicone','Plastics',['Si','O','C','H'],'Sand (silicon) + methane chemicals','A backbone of Si–O–Si instead of carbon makes it flexible and heat-proof.','Baking moulds, sealants, phone cases, medical tubes'],
['rubber','🛞','Tyre rubber','Plastics',['C','H','S'],'Natural rubber (tree sap) or synthetic rubber + sulfur + carbon black','Heating with sulfur links the chains together (vulcanisation), so the rubber stays springy.','Tyres, shoe soles, hoses'],
['cfibre','🚴','Carbon fibre','Plastics',['C','H','N','O'],'Polyacrylonitrile fibres + epoxy resin','Fibres are baked until only carbon is left, then woven and set in resin.','Racing bikes, aircraft, cars, tennis rackets'],
['chip','💻','Computer chip','Electronics & energy',['Si','O','B','P','Cu','Hf'],'Ultra-pure silicon crystal','Tiny amounts of boron and phosphorus are added (doping) to make switches; billions of transistors are printed with light and wired with copper.','Phones, computers, cars, everything electronic'],
['solar','☀️','Solar panel','Electronics & energy',['Si','B','P','Ag','Al'],'Purified silicon + silver paste + aluminium + glass','Silicon wafers are doped to make a junction that pushes electrons when light hits it.','Electricity from sunlight'],
['liion','🔋','Lithium-ion battery','Electronics & energy',['Li','Co','Ni','Mn','C','Cu','Al'],'Lithium metal oxide cathode, graphite anode, liquid electrolyte','Lithium ions move from the graphite to the metal oxide when you use it, and back when you charge.','Phones, laptops, electric cars, home storage'],
['leadacid','🚗','Car battery (lead–acid)','Electronics & energy',['Pb','S','O','H'],'Lead plates + lead dioxide + sulfuric acid','Both plates turn into lead sulfate as it gives electricity; charging reverses it. About 99% are recycled.','Starting cars, backup power'],
['led','💡','White LED','Electronics & energy',['Ga','In','N','Y','Al','Ce'],'Gallium-indium nitride chip + yellow phosphor','The chip makes blue light; a coating of cerium-doped yttrium aluminium garnet turns part of it yellow, which looks white.','Lamps, screens, torches'],
['magnet','🧲','Neodymium magnet','Electronics & energy',['Nd','Fe','B','Dy'],'Neodymium + iron + boron (+ dysprosium)','Powder is pressed in a magnetic field, baked, then magnetised.','Electric car motors, wind turbines, headphones, phones'],
['screen','📱','Touchscreen','Electronics & energy',['In','Sn','O','Si','Al','K'],'Toughened glass + indium tin oxide film','A see-through conducting layer of ITO senses your finger; the glass is toughened by swapping sodium for bigger potassium ions.','Phones, tablets, ATMs'],
['fibre','🌐','Fibre-optic cable','Electronics & energy',['Si','O','Ge','Er'],'Ultra-pure silica glass with germanium','Germanium in the core bends light back in; erbium amplifiers boost the signal on long cables.','The internet, TV cable, phone networks'],
['catalytic','💨','Catalytic converter','Electronics & energy',['Pt','Pd','Rh','Ce','Al'],'Ceramic honeycomb coated with platinum, palladium and rhodium','The metals help poisonous exhaust gases (CO, NOₓ) turn into CO₂, N₂ and water.','Cleaning car exhaust'],
['mri','🩻','MRI magnet','Electronics & energy',['Nb','Ti','He','Cu'],'Niobium–titanium wire cooled by liquid helium','Cooled to −269 °C, the wire loses all electrical resistance and makes a very strong magnetic field.','Hospital body scans'],
['fert','🌾','NPK fertiliser','Everyday chemicals',['N','P','K'],'Ammonia (from air), phosphate rock, potash','Nitrogen from air is turned into ammonia (Haber–Bosch); phosphate rock is treated with acid; potash is mined.','Growing food — it feeds billions of people'],
['soap','🧼','Soap','Everyday chemicals',['Na','C','H','O'],'Fat or oil + sodium hydroxide','Saponification: the alkali splits fats into soap molecules and glycerol.','Washing hands, bodies and clothes'],
['toothpaste','🪥','Toothpaste','Everyday chemicals',['Ca','F','Na','Si','O'],'Calcium carbonate or silica + sodium fluoride + detergent','A gentle scrub plus fluoride that hardens enamel.','Cleaning teeth and preventing decay'],
['gunpowder','🎆','Gunpowder & fireworks','Everyday chemicals',['K','N','O','C','S','Sr','Ba','Cu'],'Potassium nitrate + charcoal + sulfur, plus metal salts for colour','Saltpetre supplies oxygen so the fuel burns very fast. Strontium gives red, barium green, copper blue, sodium yellow.','Fireworks, mining, flares'],
['paper','📄','Paper','Everyday chemicals',['C','H','O','Ca','Ti'],'Wood cellulose + chalk or clay + titanium dioxide','Wood is broken into fibres with chemicals, bleached, mixed with fillers and pressed into sheets.','Books, packaging, tissues'],
['bone','🦷','Bones & teeth','Body',['Ca','P','O','H','C','N'],'Hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate) + collagen protein','Your body builds a mineral–protein composite: the mineral is hard, the collagen stops it shattering.','Your skeleton — living material that repairs itself'],
['dna','🧬','DNA','Body',['C','H','O','N','P'],'Sugar + phosphate + four bases','Cells copy DNA letter by letter using enzymes.','Storing the instructions of every living thing'],
['air','🌬️','Air','Body',['N','O','Ar','C','H'],'78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.9% argon, 0.04% CO₂, water vapour','Mostly made by volcanoes long ago; the oxygen was added by plants and algae.','Breathing, burning, flying']
];

// Per element: [where it is found, main uses, forms, compounds "formula=text|…", did-you-know]
window.ELEMENT_TEXT={
H:['The most common element in the universe (about 74% of normal matter) — it fuels the stars. On Earth it is mostly locked in water and in living things; hydrogen gas is made from natural gas or by splitting water.','Making ammonia for fertiliser; refining oil; making margarine (hardening oils); rocket fuel; fuel cells that turn hydrogen into electricity with only water as waste.','Hydrogen gas H₂ (colourless, lightest gas)|Isotopes: protium ¹H (99.98%), deuterium ²H (heavy hydrogen, in heavy water), tritium ³H (radioactive, used in glowing signs)|Metallic hydrogen — predicted at crushing pressures inside Jupiter','H2O=Water — covers 71% of Earth and makes up about 60% of your body|NH3=Ammonia — made from nitrogen and hydrogen; the base of most fertilisers|CH4=Methane — natural gas|HCl=Hydrochloric acid — in your stomach and used to clean steel|H2O2=Hydrogen peroxide — bleach and disinfectant','Hydrogen atoms made in the Big Bang 13.8 billion years ago are in the water you drink today.'],
He:['Second most common element in the universe. On Earth it comes from radioactive decay underground and is collected from natural gas wells.','Cooling MRI scanner magnets (liquid helium, −269 °C); party and weather balloons; deep-sea diving gas mixes; protecting welds; leak detection.','Helium gas — so light it escapes Earth’s atmosphere into space|Liquid helium — flows with zero friction below −271 °C (a superfluid)','—=Almost no compounds — it is a noble gas with a full outer shell','Helium was found in the Sun (by its light) 27 years before it was found on Earth — its name comes from Helios, the Greek Sun god.'],
Li:['Salt lakes (brines) in Chile, Argentina and Bolivia and rock ore (spodumene) in Australia.','Rechargeable lithium-ion batteries in phones, laptops and electric cars; heat-resistant glass and ceramics; lithium grease; medicine for bipolar disorder.','Soft silvery metal — so light it floats on oil and can be cut with a knife','Li2CO3=Lithium carbonate — raw material for batteries and a mood-stabilising medicine|LiCoO2=Lithium cobalt oxide — the positive side of many phone batteries|LiOH=Lithium hydroxide — removes CO₂ from air in spacecraft and submarines','Lithium is the lightest metal of all and one of only three elements made in the Big Bang.'],
Be:['The minerals beryl (emerald and aquamarine are forms of beryl) and bertrandite.','Light, stiff parts for aircraft and satellites; mirrors of the James Webb Space Telescope; X-ray windows; non-sparking copper-beryllium tools.','Hard, grey, very light metal','BeO=Beryllium oxide — ceramic that conducts heat like a metal|Be3Al2Si6O18=Beryl — the gem mineral; green = emerald, blue = aquamarine','Beryllium dust is toxic to breathe, so it is machined with great care.'],
B:['Borax and other borate minerals from dry lake beds in Turkey and California.','Heat-resistant borosilicate glass (lab glass, oven dishes); fibreglass insulation; detergents; strong neodymium magnets (Nd₂Fe₁₄B); control rods in nuclear reactors; plant fertiliser.','Black shiny metalloid; very hard','H3BO3=Boric acid — mild antiseptic and insecticide|Na2B4O7=Borax — cleaning powder and slime ingredient|BN=Boron nitride — almost as hard as diamond; also a slippery white lubricant form|B4C=Boron carbide — used in body armour','Boron is needed by plants to build cell walls.'],
C:['In all living things, coal, oil, natural gas, limestone and chalk (CaCO₃), and carbon dioxide in the air.','Fuels; steel (iron + a little carbon); pencils; tyres (carbon black); water filters (activated carbon); carbon-fibre bikes and planes; battery electrodes; the basis of all plastics and medicines.','Diamond — each atom bonded to 4 others in a 3-D grid; the hardest natural material|Graphite — flat sheets that slide over each other; soft, conducts electricity (pencil “lead”)|Graphene — a single sheet one atom thick; very strong and conductive|Fullerenes (C₆₀ “buckyballs”) and carbon nanotubes|Amorphous carbon — soot, charcoal, carbon black','CO2=Carbon dioxide — what we breathe out and plants take in|CO=Carbon monoxide — poisonous gas from poor burning|CH4=Methane — natural gas|CaCO3=Calcium carbonate — limestone, chalk, marble and seashells|C6H12O6=Glucose — sugar that fuels your cells|SiC=Silicon carbide — very hard; used in sandpaper and electric-car chips','Carbon forms more compounds than all other elements put together — many millions are known. That is why there is a whole branch called organic chemistry.'],
N:['78% of the air we breathe is nitrogen gas (N₂). Also in all proteins and DNA.','Making ammonia and fertilisers; liquid nitrogen for freezing food and medical samples; filling food packs to keep them fresh; explosives; inflating tyres.','Nitrogen gas N₂ — two atoms held by a very strong triple bond, so it is unreactive|Liquid nitrogen — boils at −196 °C','NH3=Ammonia — fertiliser and cleaners|HNO3=Nitric acid — for fertilisers and explosives|N2O=Nitrous oxide — laughing gas, used as a painkiller|NH4NO3=Ammonium nitrate — fertiliser|NO2=Nitrogen dioxide — brown polluting gas from car engines','Lightning and certain soil bacteria can break the strong N≡N bond — before fertiliser factories, that was the only way nitrogen reached plants.'],
O:['21% of air; 89% of the mass of water; nearly half the mass of Earth’s crust (in rocks like quartz, SiO₂). Most oxygen in air was made by plants and algae.','Breathing support in hospitals; steel making; welding and cutting torches; rocket fuel (liquid oxygen); water treatment.','Oxygen gas O₂ — what we breathe|Ozone O₃ — three atoms; the ozone layer blocks harmful UV light|Liquid oxygen — pale blue and attracted to magnets','H2O=Water|CO2=Carbon dioxide|SiO2=Silicon dioxide — sand and quartz|Fe2O3=Iron(III) oxide — rust|Al2O3=Aluminium oxide — ruby, sapphire and the protective skin on aluminium','Every cell in your body uses oxygen to “burn” food and release energy.'],
F:['The minerals fluorite (CaF₂) and cryolite.','Fluoride in toothpaste and drinking water to protect teeth; non-stick pans (PTFE/Teflon); refrigerants; making uranium fuel; medicines (about 1 in 5 drugs contains fluorine).','Pale yellow gas F₂ — the most reactive element of all','NaF=Sodium fluoride — in toothpaste|CaF2=Calcium fluoride — fluorite mineral; lenses|(C2F4)n=PTFE (Teflon) — non-stick coatings|HF=Hydrofluoric acid — etches glass; very dangerous|SF6=Sulfur hexafluoride — insulating gas in electrical switchgear','Fluorine is the most electronegative element: it pulls electrons harder than anything else. It even reacts with glass.'],
Ne:['A tiny part of air (0.0018%); separated from liquid air.','Bright red-orange neon signs; lasers (helium–neon lasers); high-voltage indicators.','Colourless noble gas that glows red-orange in an electric discharge','—=No stable compounds known','Only the red-orange signs are pure neon; other colours use other gases or coated glass.'],
Na:['The 6th most common element in Earth’s crust; the oceans are full of dissolved sodium chloride (salt).','Table salt; making glass and soap; baking soda; street lights (yellow sodium lamps); coolant in some nuclear reactors; sodium-ion batteries.','Soft silvery metal that reacts violently with water, so it is stored in oil','NaCl=Sodium chloride — table salt|NaHCO3=Sodium bicarbonate — baking soda|Na2CO3=Sodium carbonate — washing soda; used to make glass|NaOH=Sodium hydroxide — caustic soda; makes soap and paper|NaClO=Sodium hypochlorite — household bleach','Your nerves send signals by moving sodium and potassium ions in and out of cells.'],
Mg:['Seawater, and minerals like dolomite and magnesite. 8th most common element in the crust.','Light alloys for cars, laptops and bikes; fireworks and flares (burns with brilliant white light); antacids; Epsom salts; the centre of chlorophyll in plants.','Light silvery-grey metal','MgO=Magnesium oxide — heat-resistant lining for furnaces|Mg(OH)2=Magnesium hydroxide — milk of magnesia (antacid)|MgSO4=Magnesium sulfate — Epsom salts|MgCl2=Magnesium chloride — de-icing roads; source of Mg metal','Every chlorophyll molecule — the green pigment that captures sunlight — has a magnesium atom at its centre.'],
Al:['The most common metal in Earth’s crust (8%), mined as bauxite ore.','Drink cans, foil and packaging; aeroplanes, cars and bikes; window frames; power lines; kitchenware.','Light, silvery metal protected by a thin natural oxide skin, so it does not rust','Al2O3=Aluminium oxide — alumina; ruby and sapphire are coloured forms; sandpaper|Al2(SO4)3=Aluminium sulfate — cleans drinking water|AlCl3=Aluminium chloride — in antiperspirants','Aluminium was once more valuable than gold — Napoleon III served honoured guests on aluminium plates. Recycling a can saves about 95% of the energy needed to make new aluminium.'],
Si:['Second most common element in the crust (28%) — sand, quartz and most rocks are silicates.','Computer chips and solar cells (ultra-pure silicon); glass (from sand); concrete; silicone rubbers, sealants and cookware; aluminium alloys.','Hard, shiny blue-grey metalloid crystal — a semiconductor','SiO2=Silicon dioxide — sand, quartz, glass|SiC=Silicon carbide — very hard abrasive and power-electronics chips|(Si(CH3)2O)n=Silicone (PDMS) — flexible rubber, sealants, breast implants|Na2SiO3=Sodium silicate — “water glass” glue','A modern chip can hold tens of billions of tiny silicon switches (transistors) on an area smaller than a fingernail.'],
P:['Phosphate rock (apatite) in Morocco, China and the USA. In your bones, teeth, DNA and the energy molecule ATP.','Fertilisers (the main use); detergents; safety matches (red phosphorus); food additives; lithium iron phosphate batteries.','White phosphorus — glows in the dark and bursts into flame in air; very toxic|Red phosphorus — stable; on matchbox strikers|Black phosphorus — layered like graphite','H3PO4=Phosphoric acid — in cola drinks and fertilisers|Ca3(PO4)2=Calcium phosphate — bones, teeth and phosphate rock|Ca5(PO4)3OH=Hydroxyapatite — the mineral of tooth enamel|LiFePO4=Lithium iron phosphate — safe battery material','Phosphorus was discovered in 1669 by an alchemist boiling down about 1,500 litres of urine while hunting for gold.'],
S:['Near volcanoes and hot springs; mostly recovered from cleaning oil and natural gas.','Making sulfuric acid (the most-made industrial chemical); fertilisers; vulcanising rubber for tyres; gunpowder; fungicides; matches.','Yellow brittle solid; forms rings of 8 atoms (S₈)|Rhombic and monoclinic crystals, and rubbery “plastic sulfur” when melted and cooled fast','H2SO4=Sulfuric acid — car batteries, fertilisers, industry|SO2=Sulfur dioxide — acid rain gas; food preservative|H2S=Hydrogen sulfide — smells of rotten eggs|CaSO4=Calcium sulfate — gypsum and plaster of Paris|FeS2=Iron pyrite — “fool’s gold”','The smell of eggs, onions and garlic all comes from sulfur compounds.'],
Cl:['Seawater and underground salt deposits (as chloride).','Disinfecting drinking water and swimming pools; bleach; PVC plastic; making medicines and pesticides.','Yellow-green poisonous gas Cl₂','NaCl=Sodium chloride — table salt|HCl=Hydrochloric acid — stomach acid; cleaning metal|(C2H3Cl)n=PVC — pipes, window frames, cable coating|NaClO=Sodium hypochlorite — bleach|KCl=Potassium chloride — fertiliser and salt substitute','Chlorinating drinking water is one of the biggest public-health wins in history — it stopped diseases like cholera and typhoid.'],
Ar:['About 0.93% of air — the third most common gas in the atmosphere.','Shielding gas for welding; filling light bulbs; double-glazed windows (insulation); keeping old documents and wine from oxidising.','Colourless unreactive noble gas','—=No stable compounds at room conditions','“Argon” means “the lazy one” in Greek, because it does not react with anything.'],
K:['Potash minerals from ancient dried-up seas (Canada, Russia, Belarus); in bananas, potatoes and beans.','Fertilisers (about 95% of use); soaps; glass; food salt substitute.','Soft silvery metal — reacts with water even faster than sodium, with a lilac flame','KCl=Potassium chloride — fertiliser|KNO3=Potassium nitrate — saltpetre; gunpowder and fertiliser|KOH=Potassium hydroxide — soft soaps and alkaline batteries|KMnO4=Potassium permanganate — purple disinfectant','Your heartbeat depends on the right amount of potassium in your blood.'],
Ca:['Limestone, chalk and marble (CaCO₃), gypsum; the 5th most common element in the crust. 99% of the calcium in your body is in bones and teeth.','Cement and concrete; lime for steel making and farming; plaster; chalk; dietary supplements.','Soft grey metal (reacts with water)','CaCO3=Calcium carbonate — limestone, chalk, marble, eggshells, seashells|CaO=Calcium oxide — quicklime; key to cement|Ca(OH)2=Calcium hydroxide — slaked lime; mortar|CaSO4=Calcium sulfate — gypsum, plaster of Paris, plasterboard|Ca3(PO4)2=Calcium phosphate — bones','Calcium also tells your muscles when to contract and helps blood to clot.'],
Sc:['Spread thinly through many minerals; mostly a by-product of uranium and rare-earth mining.','Light, strong aluminium–scandium alloys for bike frames and aircraft; bright stadium lights (metal-halide lamps); solid-oxide fuel cells.','Soft silvery metal','Sc2O3=Scandium oxide — for lamps and fuel cells','Mendeleev predicted scandium (he called it “eka-boron”) before it was discovered.'],
Ti:['The 9th most common element in the crust — ores rutile and ilmenite (beach sands).','White paint, sunscreen, toothpaste and paper (titanium dioxide); aircraft and spacecraft; hip and dental implants; bikes; watches.','Strong, light silvery metal that does not corrode','TiO2=Titanium dioxide — the brightest white pigment; in sunscreen|TiCl4=Titanium tetrachloride — used to make pure Ti and smoke screens|TiN=Titanium nitride — gold-coloured hard coating on drills','Titanium is as strong as steel but 45% lighter, and the body accepts it — bone even grows onto it.'],
V:['Ores vanadinite and magnetite; also in crude oil.','Tough vanadium steel for tools, springs and gears; vanadium flow batteries for storing power from wind and solar.','Hard silvery-grey metal','V2O5=Vanadium pentoxide — catalyst for making sulfuric acid|VOSO4=Vanadyl sulfate — used in flow batteries','Vanadium compounds show four beautiful colours (violet, green, blue and yellow) as they change charge.'],
Cr:['The ore chromite, mainly from South Africa and Kazakhstan.','Stainless steel (at least 10.5% chromium); shiny chrome plating; pigments; leather tanning.','Hard, shiny, steel-grey metal','Cr2O3=Chromium(III) oxide — green pigment|K2Cr2O7=Potassium dichromate — orange oxidiser (toxic)|PbCrO4=Lead chromate — old “chrome yellow” paint (toxic)','Rubies are red and emeralds are green because of tiny amounts of chromium.'],
Mn:['Ores pyrolusite and rhodochrosite (South Africa, Australia, Gabon); nodules on the deep sea floor.','Steel making (removes sulfur and oxygen and adds toughness); alkaline and lithium batteries; aluminium drink cans.','Hard, brittle silvery metal','MnO2=Manganese dioxide — in ordinary alkaline batteries|KMnO4=Potassium permanganate — disinfectant|MnSO4=Manganese sulfate — fertiliser and feed supplement','Your body needs a little manganese for enzymes that build bones and protect cells.'],
Fe:['The 4th most common element in the crust and most of Earth’s core. Ores haematite (Fe₂O₃) and magnetite (Fe₃O₄).','Steel for buildings, bridges, cars, ships and tools (about 95% of all metal used); cast iron; magnets; iron supplements.','Alpha iron (ferrite) — magnetic form at room temperature|Gamma iron (austenite) — above 912 °C; dissolves carbon, which makes hardening steel possible|Wrought iron, cast iron (2–4% carbon) and steel (up to ~2% carbon)','Fe2O3=Iron(III) oxide — rust; haematite ore; red pigment|Fe3O4=Magnetite — naturally magnetic mineral|FeSO4=Iron(II) sulfate — iron tablets for anaemia|FeS2=Pyrite — fool’s gold','Haemoglobin in your red blood cells uses iron to carry oxygen — that is why blood is red.'],
Co:['Mostly from the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as a by-product of copper and nickel mining.','Lithium-ion battery cathodes; super-alloys for jet engines; strong magnets; blue glass and ceramics.','Hard, shiny, magnetic grey metal','LiCoO2=Lithium cobalt oxide — battery cathode|CoAl2O4=Cobalt blue — pigment|CoCl2=Cobalt(II) chloride — turns from blue to pink with water (humidity tests)','Vitamin B12 has a cobalt atom at its heart.'],
Ni:['Ores in Indonesia, the Philippines, Russia and Canada; much of Earth’s core is iron–nickel.','Stainless steel; batteries (nickel-rich cathodes, NiMH); coins; plating; super-alloys for turbines.','Silvery, magnetic, corrosion-resistant metal','NiO=Nickel oxide — ceramics and batteries|NiSO4=Nickel sulfate — plating and battery making|NiTi=Nitinol — “memory metal” that springs back to its shape when warmed','Many coins are copper–nickel. Some people are allergic to nickel in jewellery.'],
Cu:['Ores chalcopyrite and malachite (Chile, Peru, Congo); sometimes found as pure metal.','Electrical wires and motors (second-best conductor after silver); water pipes; brass and bronze; coins; roofs.','Reddish-orange soft metal; turns green (patina) outdoors','CuSO4=Copper(II) sulfate — bright blue crystals; kills algae and fungi|Cu2(OH)2CO3=Malachite — the green patina on the Statue of Liberty and a gem|CuO=Copper(II) oxide — black pigment','Copper was the first metal people worked, over 10,000 years ago. Mixing it with tin started the Bronze Age.'],
Zn:['Ore sphalerite (zinc blende, ZnS), mined in China, Peru and Australia.','Galvanising steel so it does not rust; brass (copper + zinc); batteries; sunscreen and nappy cream (zinc oxide); dietary supplement.','Bluish-silver brittle metal','ZnO=Zinc oxide — sunscreen, rubber, paint|ZnS=Zinc sulfide — ore; glows in the dark when doped|ZnSO4=Zinc sulfate — supplement and fertiliser','More than 300 enzymes in your body need zinc — it helps healing and taste.'],
Ga:['A by-product of making aluminium (bauxite) and zinc.','LEDs, laser diodes and fast chips (gallium nitride and gallium arsenide); phone chargers; solar cells for spacecraft.','Silvery metal that melts at 29.8 °C — it melts in your hand','GaN=Gallium nitride — blue and white LEDs, fast chargers|GaAs=Gallium arsenide — solar cells and radio chips','Gallium can make aluminium fall apart by seeping between its crystals.'],
Ge:['By-product of zinc ores and coal ash.','Fibre-optic cables; infrared lenses for night vision; early transistors; solar cells.','Grey-white shiny metalloid; a semiconductor','GeO2=Germanium dioxide — makes glass that bends light strongly (fibre optics)','The first transistor (1947) was made of germanium.'],
As:['Minerals like arsenopyrite; a by-product of copper and lead smelting.','Semiconductors (gallium arsenide); hardening lead in car batteries; formerly pesticides and wood preservatives.','Grey metallic form (most stable)|Yellow arsenic (unstable, soft)','As2O3=Arsenic trioxide — historic poison; now a leukaemia medicine|GaAs=Gallium arsenide — semiconductors','Arsenic in groundwater is a serious health problem in parts of Bangladesh and India.'],
Se:['By-product of copper refining.','Glass making (removes green tint or makes it red); solar cells; photocopier drums (formerly); anti-dandruff shampoo; dietary supplement.','Grey metallic selenium (conducts more in light)|Red amorphous selenium','SeS2=Selenium disulfide — anti-dandruff shampoo|CdSe=Cadmium selenide — quantum dots in some TVs','Brazil nuts are very rich in selenium — just one or two a day covers your need.'],
Br:['Salt lakes and seawater (the Dead Sea is very rich).','Flame retardants; drilling fluids; water treatment in hot tubs; film photography (silver bromide).','Dark red-brown liquid — one of only two elements liquid at room temperature','AgBr=Silver bromide — light-sensitive; old photographic film|NaBr=Sodium bromide — hot-tub disinfection|CH3Br=Bromomethane — old pesticide, banned for harming the ozone layer','The ancient purple dye Tyrian purple, worn by emperors, is a bromine compound made from sea snails.'],
Kr:['About 1 part per million of air.','Energy-saving lighting and camera flashes; insulating windows; lasers for chip making.','Colourless noble gas; glows whitish in a tube','KrF2=Krypton difluoride — one of the few compounds of krypton','From 1960 to 1983, the metre was defined by the wavelength of light from krypton-86.'],
Rb:['Traces in minerals like lepidolite; by-product of lithium mining.','Atomic clocks (in GPS satellites and phone networks); research lasers.','Very soft silvery metal; ignites in air','RbCl=Rubidium chloride — used in biology research','Rubidium was found by its two bright red lines of light — “rubidus” means deep red.'],
Sr:['Minerals celestine and strontianite.','Red fireworks and flares; glow-in-the-dark paints (strontium aluminate); toothpaste for sensitive teeth; old TV screens.','Soft silvery-yellow metal','SrCO3=Strontium carbonate — red colour in fireworks|SrAl2O4=Strontium aluminate — glows in the dark for hours|SrCl2=Strontium chloride — sensitive-teeth toothpaste','Radioactive strontium-90 from nuclear fallout can end up in bones because the body treats it like calcium.'],
Y:['Rare-earth minerals such as monazite and xenotime.','Red and white LEDs and phosphors; lasers (Nd:YAG); super-strong ceramics (yttria-stabilised zirconia) for dental crowns; superconductors.','Silvery metal','Y2O3=Yttrium oxide — phosphors and ceramics|YBa2Cu3O7=YBCO — a superconductor above the temperature of liquid nitrogen','Four elements (yttrium, ytterbium, terbium and erbium) are named after one Swedish village: Ytterby.'],
Zr:['Zircon (ZrSiO₄) in beach sands.','Nuclear fuel rod casing (lets neutrons pass); ceramic knives and dental crowns; fake diamonds (cubic zirconia).','Hard, silvery, corrosion-resistant metal','ZrO2=Zirconium dioxide — cubic zirconia gems; ceramic implants|ZrSiO4=Zircon — the oldest known minerals on Earth, 4.4 billion years old','Tiny zircon crystals from Australia are the oldest known pieces of Earth.'],
Nb:['Pyrochlore ore, mostly from Brazil.','Strong, light steel for pipelines and cars; superconducting magnets in MRI scanners and particle colliders (niobium–titanium); jet engines.','Soft grey metal that turns blue or purple when anodised (jewellery)','NbTi=Niobium–titanium — superconducting wire|LiNbO3=Lithium niobate — optical and phone filter crystals','The Large Hadron Collider uses about 1,200 tonnes of niobium–titanium superconducting cable.'],
Mo:['Ore molybdenite (MoS₂), often a by-product of copper mining.','Super-strong and heat-resistant steels (tools, pipelines, engines); catalysts in oil refining; lubricants.','Silvery metal with a very high melting point (2,623 °C)','MoS2=Molybdenum disulfide — slippery lubricant, like graphite','Plants and bacteria need molybdenum to turn nitrogen into proteins.'],
Tc:['Almost none in nature — it is radioactive. Made in nuclear reactors.','Medical scans: technetium-99m is used in tens of millions of scans a year to image bones, heart and organs.','Silvery-grey radioactive metal','NaTcO4=Sodium pertechnetate — the starting chemical for medical scans','Technetium was the first element made artificially (1937) — its name means “artificial”.'],
Ru:['Platinum ores in South Africa and Russia.','Hard-disk drives; long-lasting electrical contacts; chip making; catalysts; hardening platinum jewellery.','Hard white metal of the platinum group','RuO2=Ruthenium dioxide — resistors in electronics','Ruthenium is named after Ruthenia, the Latin name for Russia.'],
Rh:['Platinum ores; mostly South Africa.','Catalytic converters in cars (cleans nitrogen oxides from exhaust); shiny plating on white-gold jewellery; searchlight mirrors.','Silvery, very hard, very rare metal','RhCl3=Rhodium(III) chloride — starting material for catalysts','Rhodium is one of the rarest and most expensive metals — often pricier than gold.'],
Pd:['Nickel and platinum ores in Russia and South Africa.','Catalytic converters (most of all palladium goes here); electronics; dentistry; white gold; hydrogen purification.','Silvery-white soft metal','PdCl2=Palladium(II) chloride — catalyst for making plastics and medicines','Palladium can soak up 900 times its own volume of hydrogen gas.'],
Ag:['Silver ores, often with lead, zinc and copper (Mexico, Peru, China); sometimes as pure metal.','Jewellery, coins and silverware; solar panels; electronics (best conductor of all metals); mirrors; antibacterial wound dressings.','Shiny white precious metal','AgNO3=Silver nitrate — antiseptic; used to make mirrors|AgBr=Silver bromide — old photographic film|Ag2S=Silver sulfide — the black tarnish on silver','Silver conducts electricity and heat better than any other element.'],
Cd:['By-product of zinc refining.','Nickel–cadmium batteries (being phased out); yellow and red pigments; solar cells (cadmium telluride); nuclear control rods.','Soft bluish-white metal; toxic','CdS=Cadmium sulfide — bright yellow pigment|CdTe=Cadmium telluride — thin-film solar panels','Cadmium is very toxic and builds up in the body, so its use is strictly limited.'],
In:['By-product of zinc refining.','Indium tin oxide (ITO) — the see-through conducting layer on every touchscreen and flat screen; solders; LEDs.','Very soft silvery metal — “cries” (squeaks) when bent','In2O3·SnO2=Indium tin oxide (ITO) — transparent conductor on screens|InP=Indium phosphide — fast fibre-optic lasers','Indium is named after the bright indigo line in its light spectrum.'],
Sn:['Ore cassiterite (SnO₂), mainly from China, Indonesia and Myanmar.','Solder in all electronics; tin-plating steel food cans; bronze (copper + tin) and pewter; window glass (float glass is made on molten tin).','White tin (β) — shiny metal above 13 °C|Grey tin (α) — crumbly powder in the cold (“tin pest”)','SnO2=Tin(IV) oxide — cassiterite ore; conductive glass coating|SnF2=Tin(II) fluoride — in toothpaste|SnCl2=Tin(II) chloride — food additive and dye fixer','When you bend a tin bar it makes a crackling sound called the “tin cry”.'],
Sb:['Ore stibnite (Sb₂S₃), mostly from China.','Flame retardants; hardening lead in car batteries and bullets; semiconductors; plastic (PET) making catalyst.','Brittle silvery-grey metalloid','Sb2S3=Stibnite — ancient eye make-up (kohl)|Sb2O3=Antimony trioxide — flame retardant','Ancient Egyptians used powdered stibnite as black eye liner.'],
Te:['By-product of copper refining; one of the rarest stable elements in the crust.','Solar panels (cadmium telluride); rewritable DVDs; thermoelectric coolers; alloys.','Silvery-white brittle metalloid','CdTe=Cadmium telluride — thin-film solar cells|Bi2Te3=Bismuth telluride — turns heat into electricity (and back)','Eating even a tiny amount of tellurium makes your breath smell of garlic for weeks.'],
I:['Seaweed, seawater and nitrate deposits in Chile; iodine brines in Japan.','Iodised salt (prevents thyroid disease); disinfectants for wounds; X-ray contrast dyes; LCD screens; cloud seeding.','Shiny black-violet solid that turns directly into violet gas (sublimes)','KI=Potassium iodide — added to salt; protects the thyroid in nuclear accidents|AgI=Silver iodide — cloud seeding to make rain|I2 in alcohol=Tincture of iodine — antiseptic','Your thyroid gland uses iodine to make the hormones that control your body’s speed.'],
Xe:['About 0.09 parts per million of air — very rare.','Car headlights and cinema projector lamps; ion engines on spacecraft; anaesthesia; lasers.','Heavy noble gas; glows blue in a tube','XeF2=Xenon difluoride — first noble-gas compounds were made with xenon (1962)|XeO3=Xenon trioxide — explosive','In 1962 xenon proved that noble gases can react after all.'],
Cs:['Mineral pollucite, mostly from Canada.','Atomic clocks — the second is defined by the caesium-133 atom; drilling fluids for oil wells; photoelectric cells.','Soft gold-coloured metal; melts at 28.5 °C; explodes in water','CsCl=Caesium chloride — used in biology labs to separate DNA|HCOOCs=Caesium formate — heavy drilling fluid','One second is exactly 9,192,631,770 ticks of a caesium atom.'],
Ba:['Mineral barite (barium sulfate).','“Barium meal” to show the gut in X-rays; heavy drilling mud for oil wells; green fireworks; glass.','Soft silvery metal','BaSO4=Barium sulfate — X-ray contrast; white paint filler|BaCO3=Barium carbonate — rat poison, ceramics|Ba(NO3)2=Barium nitrate — green fireworks','Barium sulfate is so insoluble that swallowing it is safe, even though other barium compounds are poisonous.'],
La:['Rare-earth ores monazite and bastnäsite (China, USA, Australia).','Camera and telescope lenses; nickel–metal-hydride batteries in hybrid cars; oil-refining catalysts; lighter flints.','Soft silvery metal','La2O3=Lanthanum oxide — high-quality lens glass|LaNi5=Lanthanum nickel — stores hydrogen in batteries','A Toyota Prius battery contains around 10–15 kg of lanthanum.'],
Ce:['The most common rare-earth element; ores monazite and bastnäsite.','Polishing glass and lenses (cerium oxide); catalytic converters; self-cleaning ovens; lighter flints (with iron).','Soft iron-grey metal; sparks when scratched','CeO2=Cerium(IV) oxide — glass polish and catalyst','The sparks from a lighter flint come from cerium alloy.'],
Pr:['Rare-earth ores.','Strong magnets (with neodymium); yellow-green glass and ceramics; welders’ goggles; aircraft engine alloys.','Soft silvery metal','Pr6O11=Praseodymium oxide — yellow ceramic glazes','Praseodymium and neodymium were once thought to be one element called “didymium” (twin).'],
Nd:['Rare-earth ores, mostly from China.','The strongest permanent magnets (Nd₂Fe₁₄B) in electric car motors, wind turbines, headphones and hard drives; lasers.','Silvery metal that tarnishes quickly','Nd2Fe14B=Neodymium magnet — tiny but very strong|Nd:YAG=Neodymium-doped yttrium aluminium garnet — powerful lasers','A large wind turbine can contain hundreds of kilograms of neodymium.'],
Pm:['Almost none in nature — it is radioactive; made in nuclear reactors.','Nuclear batteries for pacemakers and spacecraft (formerly); luminous paint; thickness gauges.','Radioactive metal','Pm2O3=Promethium oxide — used in atomic batteries','Named after Prometheus, who stole fire from the gods in Greek myth.'],
Sm:['Rare-earth ores.','Samarium–cobalt magnets that work when hot (guitars, military, space); cancer pain treatment; nuclear control rods.','Silvery metal','SmCo5=Samarium–cobalt — heat-proof magnets','The first element named after a person (the mineral samarskite was named after a Russian mining official).'],
Eu:['Rare-earth ores.','Red and blue phosphors in screens and energy-saving lamps; anti-forgery marks on euro banknotes (glow under UV).','Most reactive rare-earth metal','Eu2O3=Europium oxide — red phosphor','Put a euro note under UV light and the glowing symbols contain europium.'],
Gd:['Rare-earth ores.','MRI contrast agents; neutron shielding; data storage; magnetic refrigeration research.','Silvery metal; magnetic below about 20 °C','Gd-DTPA=Gadolinium contrast agent — makes MRI images brighter','Gadolinium is magnetic on a cold day but not on a warm one.'],
Tb:['Rare-earth ores.','Green phosphors in screens and lamps; magnets for electric motors; sonar and actuators (Terfenol-D).','Silvery metal','Tb2O3=Terbium oxide — green phosphor|Terfenol-D=Terbium-dysprosium-iron — changes shape in a magnetic field','One of four elements named after Ytterby in Sweden.'],
Dy:['Rare-earth ores (ionic clays in China).','Added to neodymium magnets so they keep working when hot (electric cars, wind turbines); lasers; lighting.','Silvery metal','Dy2O3=Dysprosium oxide — used in ceramics and magnets','Its name means “hard to get at” in Greek — it took chemists many tries to separate it.'],
Ho:['Rare-earth ores.','Medical lasers (kidney stones, eye surgery); the strongest magnetic fields (pole pieces); nuclear control rods.','Silvery metal','Ho2O3=Holmium oxide — calibrates light meters (spectrometers)','Holmium has the highest magnetic strength of any element.'],
Er:['Rare-earth ores.','Amplifiers in fibre-optic internet cables; pink glass and gems; skin and dental lasers.','Silvery metal','Er2O3=Erbium oxide — pink colouring for glass','Erbium-doped fibre amplifiers boost the light signals that carry the internet under the oceans.'],
Tm:['Rare-earth ores; one of the rarest.','Portable X-ray machines; surgical lasers; euro banknote security (blue glow).','Silvery-grey metal','Tm2O3=Thulium oxide — laser material','Named after Thule, an ancient name for the far north (Scandinavia).'],
Yb:['Rare-earth ores.','Ultra-precise atomic clocks; lasers; stainless steel additive; earthquake stress gauges.','Soft silvery metal','Yb2O3=Ytterbium oxide — lasers and ceramics','Ytterbium atomic clocks are so precise they would lose less than a second in the age of the universe.'],
Lu:['Rare-earth ores; the heaviest and hardest rare earth.','PET scanner crystals; cancer treatment (lutetium-177 therapy); oil-refining catalysts.','Silvery metal','Lu2SiO5=Lutetium oxyorthosilicate — flashes of light in PET scanners','Lutetium-177 medicines can seek out and destroy prostate-cancer cells.'],
Hf:['Always found with zirconium in zircon.','Control rods in nuclear submarines; jet-engine alloys; insulator layers inside modern computer chips.','Silvery metal','HfO2=Hafnium oxide — insulator in chip transistors','Hafnium and zirconium are so alike chemically that hafnium was not noticed until 1923.'],
Ta:['Ore coltan (columbite–tantalite), from Congo, Rwanda, Brazil and Australia.','Tiny high-capacity capacitors in phones and laptops; surgical implants; jet-engine parts; chemical equipment.','Grey, hard, very corrosion-resistant metal','Ta2O5=Tantalum pentoxide — capacitor insulator and camera-lens glass|TaC=Tantalum carbide — very hard cutting tools','Mining of coltan in conflict areas is why some companies check that their phones use “conflict-free” tantalum.'],
W:['Ores wolframite and scheelite, mainly China.','Cutting tools, drill bits and armour (tungsten carbide); old light-bulb filaments; heavy weights; welding electrodes.','Hard grey metal with the highest melting point of all metals (3,422 °C)','WC=Tungsten carbide — nearly as hard as diamond|WO3=Tungsten trioxide — smart windows that darken','Its symbol W comes from its other name, wolfram.'],
Re:['By-product of molybdenum from copper mining; one of the rarest elements.','Jet-engine turbine blades (nickel super-alloys); petrol-refining catalysts; thermocouples.','Silvery-white metal; very high melting point','ReS2=Rhenium disulfide — studied for electronics','Rhenium was the last stable element to be discovered (1925).'],
Os:['Platinum ores.','Very hard alloys for fountain-pen nibs, electrical contacts and old record-player needles; staining cells for microscopes.','Blue-grey metal — the densest element (22.6 g/cm³)','OsO4=Osmium tetroxide — stains fat in cells for microscopes (very toxic)','A tennis-ball-sized lump of osmium would weigh about 3.5 kg.'],
Ir:['Platinum ores; very rare in the crust but common in meteorites.','Spark plugs; crucibles for growing crystals; the old international kilogram and metre (platinum–iridium); chip making.','Very hard, dense, most corrosion-resistant metal','IrO2=Iridium oxide — electrodes for making hydrogen from water','A worldwide layer of iridium in rocks 66 million years old is evidence of the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.'],
Pt:['Mostly South Africa and Russia.','Catalytic converters; jewellery; lab equipment; cancer drugs (cisplatin); pacemaker parts; fuel cells; hard-disk coatings.','Silvery-white precious metal; does not tarnish','PtCl2(NH3)2=Cisplatin — an important cancer medicine|H2PtCl6=Chloroplatinic acid — makes platinum catalysts','All the platinum ever mined would fit in a cube about 8 metres on each side.'],
Au:['Gold veins in quartz, river gravels; China, Australia, Russia and the USA mine the most.','Jewellery; money and investment; reliable electronic connections; dentistry; spacecraft sun shields; some arthritis and cancer medicines.','Soft yellow precious metal that never rusts; can be beaten into leaf thinner than 0.0001 mm','HAuCl4=Chloroauric acid — makes gold nanoparticles|Au nanoparticles=Colloidal gold — ruby-red glass and rapid tests (like pregnancy tests)','All the gold ever mined would make a cube only about 22 metres on each side.'],
Hg:['Ore cinnabar (HgS), a red mineral.','Old thermometers and barometers (now mostly replaced); fluorescent lamps; dental amalgam (being phased out); gold mining (harmful).','Silvery liquid metal at room temperature','HgS=Mercury sulfide — cinnabar; the pigment vermilion|HgCl2=Mercury(II) chloride — very toxic|Hg in amalgam=Dental amalgam — mercury + silver + tin + copper','Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature. Its vapour and many compounds are toxic.'],
Tl:['By-product of zinc and lead ores.','Special glass and infrared optics; heart scans (thallium-201); electronics.','Soft grey metal; very toxic','Tl2SO4=Thallium sulfate — former rat poison, now banned','Thallium was called “the poisoner’s poison” because it is tasteless and colourless.'],
Pb:['Ore galena (PbS), in China, Australia and the USA.','Car batteries (lead–acid); radiation shielding (X-ray aprons); roofing; weights; formerly pipes, paint and petrol — now removed because lead harms the brain.','Soft, heavy, blue-grey metal','PbS=Galena — the main lead ore|PbO2=Lead dioxide — positive plate in car batteries|PbCO3=White lead — old paint (toxic)','The word “plumber” comes from plumbum, Latin for lead, because Roman water pipes were lead.'],
Bi:['By-product of lead, copper and tungsten ores.','Stomach medicines (Pepto-Bismol); low-melting safety alloys for fire sprinklers; lead-free solder and bullets; cosmetics (pearly shine).','Pinkish-silver metal that forms rainbow-coloured staircase crystals','BiOCl=Bismuth oxychloride — pearly effect in make-up|C7H5BiO4=Bismuth subsalicylate — stomach-upset medicine','Bismuth is weakly radioactive, but so slowly that it would take far longer than the age of the universe for half to decay.'],
Po:['Tiny amounts in uranium ores; made in nuclear reactors.','Anti-static brushes; heat source for space probes (formerly).','Radioactive silvery metal; extremely toxic','PoO2=Polonium dioxide','Discovered by Marie and Pierre Curie, and named after Marie’s home country, Poland.'],
At:['One of the rarest natural elements — less than 1 gram in the whole crust at any time.','Research into targeted cancer therapy (astatine-211).','Radioactive; appearance unknown because a visible amount would vaporise itself with its own heat','—=Very few compounds studied','Nobody has ever seen a visible piece of astatine.'],
Rn:['Made by decay of uranium in rocks; seeps into basements.','Formerly cancer radiotherapy; earthquake research. Mainly important as a health hazard.','Radioactive, colourless noble gas','—=Almost no compounds','Radon in homes is the second biggest cause of lung cancer after smoking — it can be tested for and removed with ventilation.'],
Fr:['Very tiny amounts in uranium ores; only a few atoms at a time.','Research only.','Radioactive alkali metal','—=Almost unknown','Francium was discovered in 1939 by Marguerite Perey, who worked in Marie Curie’s lab.'],
Ra:['Uranium ores (tiny amounts).','Formerly glow-in-the-dark watch dials (stopped after workers were harmed); radium-223 to treat bone cancer.','Radioactive, glowing silvery metal','RaCl2=Radium chloride — the form Marie Curie isolated|RaCl2 (²²³Ra)=Radium-223 dichloride — bone cancer medicine','The “Radium Girls” who painted watch dials in the 1920s helped win workers’ safety laws.'],
Ac:['Traces in uranium ores.','Actinium-225 for targeted cancer therapy; neutron sources.','Radioactive metal that glows pale blue in the dark','Ac2O3=Actinium oxide','Actinium gives the whole actinide series its name.'],
Th:['Monazite sands (India, Brazil, Australia); about as common as lead.','Possible future nuclear fuel; old gas-lamp mantles; heat-resistant ceramics; welding electrodes.','Weakly radioactive silvery metal','ThO2=Thorium dioxide — the highest melting oxide (3,350 °C)','Much of the heat inside Earth comes from the slow decay of thorium and uranium.'],
Pa:['Tiny amounts in uranium ores.','Research only.','Radioactive, shiny metal','Pa2O5=Protactinium pentoxide','One of the rarest and most expensive natural elements.'],
U:['Ores uraninite (pitchblende) — Kazakhstan, Canada and Australia mine the most.','Nuclear power (fuel rods with uranium-235); nuclear weapons; depleted uranium for armour and ship ballast; formerly yellow-green glass.','Silvery radioactive metal|Isotopes U-238 (99.3%) and U-235 (0.7%, the one that splits in reactors)','UO2=Uranium dioxide — nuclear fuel pellets|UF6=Uranium hexafluoride — gas used to enrich uranium|U3O8=“Yellowcake” — concentrated ore','One uranium fuel pellet the size of a fingertip gives as much energy as a tonne of coal.'],
Np:['Made in nuclear reactors; traces in uranium ores.','Neutron detectors; making plutonium-238 for space batteries.','Radioactive silvery metal','NpO2=Neptunium dioxide','The first element heavier than uranium ever made (1940). Named after the planet Neptune, just as uranium is named after Uranus.'],
Pu:['Made in nuclear reactors.','Nuclear weapons and reactor fuel (plutonium-239); plutonium-238 powers space probes like Voyager and Mars rovers.','Radioactive silvery metal with six different solid forms','PuO2=Plutonium dioxide — fuel for space batteries','The Voyager probes, launched in 1977, still run on plutonium heat after more than 45 years.'],
Am:['Made in nuclear reactors.','Smoke detectors (americium-241 makes the air conduct a tiny current); moisture gauges.','Radioactive silvery metal','AmO2=Americium dioxide — the form in smoke detectors','There is probably a speck of americium in the smoke alarm in your home.'],
Cm:['Made in nuclear reactors.','Power sources and X-ray tools on Mars rovers (alpha particle X-ray spectrometers).','Radioactive metal; glows purple','Cm2O3=Curium oxide','Named after Marie and Pierre Curie.'],
Bk:['Made in nuclear reactors in tiny amounts.','Research — making even heavier elements (it was used to make tennessine).','Radioactive metal','BkO2=Berkelium dioxide','Named after Berkeley, California, where it was first made.'],
Cf:['Made in nuclear reactors.','Starting up nuclear reactors; neutron scans to find gold, oil and explosives; cancer therapy.','Radioactive metal','Cf2O3=Californium oxide','One microgram of californium-252 gives off about 2 million neutrons per second.'],
Es:['Made in nuclear reactors; first found in the debris of the first hydrogen bomb (1952).','Research only.','Radioactive metal; glows blue','—=Few compounds studied','Named after Albert Einstein.'],
Fm:['First found in hydrogen-bomb debris; made in reactors.','Research only.','Radioactive metal (never seen as a visible amount)','—=Few compounds studied','Named after Enrico Fermi, who built the first nuclear reactor.'],
Md:['Made in particle accelerators, a few atoms at a time.','Research only.','Radioactive (only atoms)','—=None studied in bulk','Named after Dmitri Mendeleev, who created the periodic table.'],
No:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Radioactive (only atoms)','—=None studied in bulk','Named after Alfred Nobel, founder of the Nobel Prizes.'],
Lr:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Radioactive (only atoms)','—=None studied in bulk','Named after Ernest Lawrence, inventor of the cyclotron.'],
Rf:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Ernest Rutherford, who discovered the atomic nucleus.'],
Db:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Dubna, Russia, home of a major lab for making new elements.'],
Sg:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Glenn Seaborg — the first element named after a living person.'],
Bh:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Niels Bohr, who explained electron shells.'],
Hs:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after the German state of Hesse.'],
Mt:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Lise Meitner, who helped discover nuclear fission.'],
Ds:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Darmstadt, Germany.'],
Rg:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Wilhelm Röntgen, who discovered X-rays.'],
Cn:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — may be a liquid or gas at room temperature','—=None','Named after Nicolaus Copernicus.'],
Nh:['Made in particle accelerators in Japan.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','The first element discovered in Asia — named after Nihon (Japan).'],
Fl:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after the Flerov lab in Dubna, Russia.'],
Mc:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Moscow region.'],
Lv:['Made in particle accelerators.','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory in California.'],
Ts:['Made in particle accelerators (2010).','Research only.','Synthetic — only atoms ever made','—=None','Named after the US state of Tennessee.'],
Og:['Made in particle accelerators — only about five atoms ever detected.','Research only.','Synthetic — predicted to be a solid, unlike the other noble gases','—=None','The heaviest element known. Named after Yuri Oganessian — the second element named after a living person.']
};
